{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["The reason for <b>on a distant road</b>: Rashi wrote (Rashi on Numbers 9:10) that it means outside the threshold of the courtyard for the entire duration of the slaughtering, and this is hinted at in the vocalization according to that opinion. But I wonder at him: why did he adopt the position of Rabbi Eliezer? The correct approach is to adopt the position of Rabbi Akiva — from Modi'im and outward. And so is the view of the Amoraim in the Gemara (Pesachim 93b), for Ulla said: anyone who cannot enter at the time of slaughtering, and this is the plain sense of the verse, for one who stands at the beginning of the period between the evenings in a place from which he cannot reach the courtyard at the time of slaughtering — a distant road it is for him, and he is exempt. And it is possible that the reason for the vocalization is that it is a road distant for him at the performance of the Pesach, not distant in the literal sense, and therefore he went back and said, 'and was on a road' (Numbers 9:13), and did not mention 'distant.' And Scripture commanded the impure person and the one who was on a distant road to perform the second one, and the same judgment and reasoning applies to everyone who did not perform the first one, even if intentionally, who is obligated to perform the second one according to the words of our Sages (Pesachim there). And Scripture mentioned these only to say that they may perform the second one by license and to restrain the impure person from performing the first one. But one who was on the road is exempt from the first and performs the second, and if he wanted to discharge his obligation through the first and commanded and they slaughtered on his behalf, he is atoned for — for we maintain (Pesachim 92a) that it is merely a leniency, for the Merciful One has compassion upon him, and if he does so, a blessing will come upon him. And it is possible that this is why Scripture mentioned <b>distant</b>, for on a nearby road it is within his power to perform either the second or the first — they may slaughter and sprinkle the blood on his behalf and he may come and eat in the evening."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:03:54.021116261Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}